Title: thread_local + std::thread deadlock on destruction.
Body: <p>Anyone knows what kind of UB is this? The following code deadlocks on <code>jthread</code> destruction when built with MSVC 19.29.30148, sometimes it deadlocks after std::cout and sometimes before.
This is somehow related to thread_local, but I cannot see what is the issue. It seems to work fine under other compilers and platforms.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;thread&gt;
#include &lt;memory&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

int main(void)
{
    std::thread t2(
        [&amp;] {
            thread_local std::jthread asd([] {
                    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;ASD&quot; &lt;&lt; std::endl;
                    });
        });
    if (t2.joinable()) { t2.join(); }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Update: Reproducible both on static and dynamic runtime</p>

answer:
import threading

def thread_local_jthread():
    # Simulate a jthread-like behavior
    print("ASD")

def main():
    thread = threading.Thread(target=thread_local_jthread)
    thread.start()
    thread.join()

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()

import threading
import ctypes

class ThreadLocalDestructor(ctypes.CDLL):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()

    def __del__(self):
        # Ensure the thread-local storage is properly cleaned up
        self.libfree(self.handle)

def f():
    global thread_local
    thread_local = threading.local()
    thread_local.ptr = ctypes.c_int(0)
    ctypes.pythonapi.Py_AddPendingCall(thread_local_free, thread_local)

def thread_local_free(arg):
    del arg.ptr

if __name__ == "__main__":
    t = threading.Thread(target=f)
    t.start()
    t.join()

